For the <parametric curve> $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t^2,t^3)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, the <vector field> and the tangent vector are
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(9t^2,-14t^5,20t^7),\qquad
\mathbf r'(t)=(1,2t,3t^2).
$$
Consequently the <line integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\int_0^1(9t^2-28t^6+60t^9)\,dt
=3-4+6=5.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Compute the <line integral> on each straight segment. On the first, $y=z=0$ and only $dx$ contributes; on the second, $x=1,z=0$ and only $dy$ contributes; on the third, $x=y=1$ and only $dz$ contributes. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
&=\int_0^1 3x^2\,dx+\int_0^1 0\,dy+\int_0^1 20z^2\,dz\\
&=1+0+\frac{20}{3}=\boxed{\frac{23}{3}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Although $F_x=9$ on the second segment, that term does no <work> there because $dx=0$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Parametrize the straight segment by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Then the <line integral> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
&=\int_0^1\bigl(3t^2+6t-14t^2+20t^3\bigr)\,dt\\
&=3-\frac{11}{3}+5=\boxed{\frac{13}{3}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The three different answers are possible because this <vector field> is not a <conservative vector field>; for example $\partial_yF_x=6$ whereas $\partial_xF_y=0$.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Take counterclockwise traversal as viewed from $z>0$. In the plane $z=0$, the <work> is the planar <line integral> of $P=2x-y$ and $Q=x+y$. <Green's theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{W=\oint_C(P\,dx+Q\,dy)
=\iint_{x^2+y^2\leq9}(1-(-1))\,dx\,dy
=2\pi(3^2)=18\pi.}
$$
The $z$ component contributes nothing because $dz=0$. The question specifies one circuit without specifying its direction: clockwise traversal gives $\boxed{-18\pi}$.

